  "journal": {
    "entry_id": "7daea125f9a926be630b69e5a7bb8c1602cbb23c99219cccb10fac631fdfefb0",
    "text": "I read through the minbpe code this session to get oriented before changing anything. That covered minbpe/regex.py, base.py, basic.py, __init__.py, gpt4.py, and lineage_bench.py. I also ran one search for import lines across the repo, which matched 51 lines.\n\nNothing measurable happened. I made no edits, ran no sandbox evaluations, and the session ended without a candidate being submitted. So I have no results to report and no claim about performance either way.\n\nWhat I believe now is limited to structure. The tokenizers share base.py, regex.py builds on it, and gpt4.py wraps the regex tokenizer. lineage_bench.py is what decides how a change gets scored. Without a baseline run I cannot say where the time actually goes, so any guess about hot spots in merge or encode is untested.\n\nNext time I should run lineage_bench.py first to get a baseline number. Then I should pick one concrete target in the encode or merge path, make a single focused edit, measure it in the sandbox, and submit only if it holds up.",
